[?25l> Now let me carefully study the C code's comment check bug. In tokenizer_next_token, the comment check is:
c
if (c == '/' && (peek_char() == '/' || peek_char() == '*')) {

But c is already peek_char() — so this checks if c == '/' and then checks peek_char() again, which is the SAME character (since we haven't advanced). This means the comment branch fires when c == '/' (since peek_char() still returns /), which is actually correct for // but wrong for /* — it would need the NEXT char to be *. Let me re-read... Actually c = peek_char() at the top, and peek_char() returns input_buffer[current_position] without advancing. So c == peek_char() is always true. The comment check c == '/' && (peek_char() == '/' || peek_char() == '*') simplifies to c == '/' since peek_char() == c == '/'. This is a bug — it means ANY / triggers the comment scanner. I need to reproduce this exactly.

